            "text": "{\n  \"study_topic_id\": \"study-15be302cb9e5fb6c6a07\",\n  \"overview\": {\n    \"text\": \"Sodium (Na), element 11, is a soft, silvery metal in Group 1, the alkali metals. Its metallic form reacts vigorously with water and air, so sodium is not found as a free metal in nature. A useful clue to its identity is the intense yellow-orange light associated with sodium in flame tests; its characteristic spectral line is near 589 nanometres. Sodium is more commonly encountered in compounds: sodium chloride is table salt, sodium carbonate is soda ash, sodium bicarbonate is baking soda, and sodium hydroxide is caustic soda. Humphry Davy first isolated the metal in 1807 by using electricity to decompose sodium hydroxide.\",\n    \"source_urls\": [\n      \"https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/11/sodium\",\n      \"https://periodic.lanl.gov/11.shtml\",\n      \"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Electromagnetic_Math.pdf\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"key_facts\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Sodium’s symbol is Na, its atomic number is 11, and it belongs to Group 1; its outer-electron configuration is [Ne]3s¹.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://periodic.lanl.gov/11.shtml\",\n        \"https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/11/sodium\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Pure sodium is a soft, silvery metal that reacts vigorously with water and is not found as a free metal in nature.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/11/sodium\",\n        \"https://periodic.lanl.gov/11.shtml\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Sodium gives a distinctive yellow-orange flame-test glow; its prominent solar-spectrum line is near 589 nm.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/11/sodium\",\n        \"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Electromagnetic_Math.pdf\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Familiar sodium compounds include table salt (NaCl), soda ash (Na₂CO₃), baking soda (NaHCO₃), and caustic soda (NaOH).\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://periodic.lanl.gov/11.shtml\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Humphry Davy isolated sodium in 1807 by electrolysis of caustic soda, or sodium hydroxide.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/11/sodium\",\n        \"https://periodic.lanl.gov/11.shtml\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"sources\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Sodium - Element information, properties and uses | Periodic Table\",\n      \"url\": \"https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/11/sodium\",\n      \"publisher\": \"Royal Society of Chemistry\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24\",\n      \"supports\": \"Atomic number, group, electron configuration, physical appearance, reactivity, occurrence, flame-test color, common compounds, and Davy’s isolation of sodium.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Periodic Table of Elements: LANL — Sodium\",\n      \"url\": \"https://periodic.lanl.gov/11.shtml\",\n      \"publisher\": \"Los Alamos National Laboratory\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24\",\n      \"supports\": \"Element identity, atomic number, symbol, group-related properties, reactivity, absence as free metal in nature, major sodium compounds, and 1807 isolation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Electromagnetic Math\",\n      \"url\": \"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Electromagnetic_Math.pdf\",\n      \"publisher\": \"NASA Space Math\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24\",\n      \"supports\": \"The solar-spectrum sodium line at 589 nm and its identification among Fraunhofer lines.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"research_queries\": [\n    \"site:rsc.org periodic table sodium element properties flame test\",\n    \"site:pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov sodium element atomic number compounds\",\n    \"site:chem.libretexts.org sodium flame test yellow sodium chloride sodium hydroxide\",\n    \"site:science.nasa.gov sodium Fraunhofer D lines solar spectrum\",\n    \"site:education.jlab.org/itselemental sodium element atomic number 11 sodium compounds\",\n    \"site:edu.rsc.org \\\"Sodium Na+ (yellow flame)\\\"\",\n    \"site:periodic.lanl.gov sodium element sodium compounds yellow flame\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The overview is one original paragraph of 101 words. Claims about sodium’s flame color are phrased as yellow-orange in keeping with the opened RSC element page; NASA supports the cited 589 nm spectral-line detail.\",\n  \"unresolved_concerns\": [],\n  \"self_check_complete\": true\n}"
